# templates module

The `templates` module provides default layout templates for common targets.

## Types

### `Kind`

Built-in template kind.

Values: `linux_elf`, `windows_pe`, `macos_macho`, `bare_metal`.

- `name(self)`: return a short name such as `"linux-elf"`.

### `Template`

A complete default layout description.

Fields:

- `kind`: `Kind`
- `base_address`: preferred base virtual address
- `page_size`: page size in bytes
- `default_entry`: default entry symbol
- `default_output`: default output file name
- `preferred_sections`: preferred output section order

Constants:

- `linux_sections`: `.text`, `.rodata`, `.data`, `.bss`
- `windows_sections`: `.text`, `.rdata`, `.data`, `.bss`
- `macos_sections`: `.text`, `.const`, `.data`, `.bss`
- `bare_sections`: `.text`, `.rodata`, `.data`, `.bss`

## Functions

- `forTarget(info, bare_metal)`: build the template for a target. Bare-metal is selected when `bare_metal` is true.
- `applyBase(layout, tmpl)`: apply the template base address and page size to a layout.
- `sectionStart(layout, name)`: virtual address of the first byte of a named output section.
- `sectionEnd(layout, name)`: virtual address one past the last byte of a named output section.
- `imageEnd(layout)`: highest allocated virtual address.
- `provideBoundarySymbols(table, layout, tmpl)`: define standard boundary symbols if their names are still free.

## Boundary symbols

After address assignment, the template can provide:

- `__executable_start`
- `etext` and `_etext`
- `edata` and `_edata`
- `__bss_start`
- `end` and `_end`

These are only added when the name is not already defined.

## Example

```zig
const bare_metal = options.region_specs.items.len > 0;
const tmpl = sibl.templateForTarget(target_info, bare_metal);

var layout = sibl.Layout.init(allocator, tmpl.base_address, tmpl.page_size);
defer layout.deinit();

sibl.applyTemplateBase(&layout, tmpl);
```

## Notes

- Bare-metal is selected when any `--region` rule is present.
- Windows PE uses base `0x140000000` for 64-bit and `0x400000` for 32-bit.
- macOS Mach-O uses base `0x100000000`.
- Linux ELF uses base `0x400000`.
